  "notes": [
   "Parts are unchanged from step 1.",
   "No breadboard is used: the manual solders wires directly between the Nano Every, DS3231 module and BME280; all wires are modelled as jumper-wire with both ends soldered.",
   "DS3231MPMB1 is not in the catalogue and lookup found nothing, so it is a new type 'ds3231-rtc' with a GND/VCC/SDA/SCL header on one side and a pass-through header on the other (GND_2/VCC_2/SDA_2/SCL_2), joined internally. The second set of four wires runs from the pass-through side to the BME280.",
   "Wiring: Nano 3V3 -> RTC VCC and BME280 3V3 (red); GND -> RTC and BME280 GND (black); A4 = SDA (blue); A5 = SCL (yellow). The BME280 is a 3.3 V part, so it is powered from 3V3, not 5V.",
   "LED strip: 5V from Nano 5V pin (USB-sourced, no stress on the regulator), data on D7 (white wire, to DIN), and a GND wire (assumed; the manual only implies it). DOUT is unused.",
   "The catalogue 'led-strip' lists no ports and lookup returned none, so I used the names 5V, DIN and GND for the strip's pads (the manual calls them VDC, DIN and GND); these ports are not in the catalogue entry.",
   "The USB cable has no catalogue ports, so its end is left unspecified; it plugs into the Nano Every's USB port and supplies power.",
   "The 3D-printed gnome enclosure is not an electronic part and is omitted."
  ]
 },
